This series lets a reviewer settle a thread from the list itself, as
decided in "Re: Supporting status trailers in emails" [1]. A reply
that ends in an "Approved:" or "Rejected:" trailer tags the whole
thread approved or rejected on the next notmuch new, and the tags come
back when the database is rebuilt.

Patch 1 adds the tags to mail-tag.sh. The latest verdict on a thread
wins: the one in the latest message, and within that message the last
trailer. Neither tag is excluded from searches, since you will filter
them by hand for now. It also moves the trailer reading that
Superseded-by already did into a helper both use.

Patch 2 documents the whole trailer protocol in
zib/patch-review-workflow.org: where trailers go, what voids them, how
a value continues over several lines, and what Superseded-by,
Approved: and Rejected: each mean.

I tested patch 1 on a scratch maildir. An approval followed by a later
rejection gives rejected; a quoted "Rejected:" is ignored; a trailer
wrapped without an indent is ignored; a value continued on an indented
line and followed by a signature counts; and a second run leaves the
tags unchanged. Run over the real list, no message has a verdict yet,
so nothing is tagged.

The series applies to b/dev@upstream, on top of two unmerged series:
"[PATCH] notmuch: Tag patches reported committed" [2] and "[PATCH 0/3]
notmuch: Tag superseded series and hide them" [3]. The
prerequisite-patch-id lines below name their commits.

